\begin{abstract}
Period finding is the entire quantum content of Shor's factoring algorithm: given a modulus $N$ and a base $a$ with $\gcd(a,N)=1$, determine the multiplicative order $r$ of $a$. We give a precise account of \emph{where} the classical approach fails and \emph{what} the quantum circuit supplies in its place, in a setting where both sides use literally the same discrete Fourier transform on $\Zn$.

On the classical side we isolate two logically independent barriers. \textbf{Barrier 1 (information-theoretic).} Any scheme that determines an arbitrary period-$r$ signal from $K$ Fourier measurements at chosen frequencies must satisfy $K \ge r$; and in a finite cyclic group at most $B^2$ elements --- more sharply, at most $B \cdot \#\{d \mid |G| : d \le B\}$ --- have order at most $B$, so modulo a prime $p \ge 3$ some base has order exceeding $\lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor$, and whenever $2B^2 < p-1$ a strict majority of bases have order exceeding $B$. In the bit-size variable $x = \log N$ the resulting requirement $\sqrt N = e^{x/2}$ is superpolynomial. \textbf{Barrier 2 (structural).} Even granted $K \ge r$ samples, the value signal $x \mapsto a^x \bmod N$ is spectrally diffuse: for the textbook instance $N=15$, $a=7$ the fundamental bin has modulus $\sqrt{45}$ while the second harmonic has modulus $15$, so naive peak picking returns the period $2$, which is \emph{false}. We give an exact closed-form criterion deciding this comparison for all order-$4$ instances, verify it on four instances, and record that it holds for $684$ of the $1870$ order-$4$ pairs with $N < 500$.

On the quantum side, the state left in the input register after one coherent evaluation of $a^x \bmod N$ is the indicator of an arithmetic progression --- the \emph{coherent comb}. We compute its transform exactly: the spectrum is a perfect Dirac comb, of modulus $m = n/r$ on the $r$ frequencies divisible by $m$ and identically zero elsewhere; the peaks are equal in height; any peak $jm$ with $\gcd(j,r)=1$ determines $r$ as the denominator of $jm/n$ in lowest terms, and $\varphi(r)$ such peaks exist.

Finally we prove a Donoho--Stark discrete uncertainty principle for this transform, $\#\supp v \cdot \#\supp \hat v \ge n$ for all $v \ne 0$, and show that the coherent comb \emph{saturates} it, $m \cdot r = n$. A rigidity converse shows that spectra supported on the multiples of $m$ are exactly the $r$-periodic signals. Thus the quantum input state is extremal for the time--frequency trade-off: no input, classical or quantum, is sharper. We are explicit about scope: nothing here lower-bounds classical \emph{factoring} time.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} period finding; discrete Fourier transform; coherent comb; Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle; multiplicative order; spectral hiding; quantum--classical separation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The reduction and the question}

Let $N$ be composite, $a$ a base with $\gcd(a,N)=1$, and $r = \ord_N(a)$ the least positive integer with $a^r \equiv 1 \pmod N$. For a random $a$, with constant probability $r$ is even and $a^{r/2}\not\equiv -1$, whence $\gcd(a^{r/2}-1,N)$ is a nontrivial factor. Every other step of Shor's algorithm is classical and cheap. The quantum content of factoring is therefore exactly:
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Period finding.} Determine $r$ from access to $x \mapsto a^x \bmod N$.
\end{quote}

The standard narrative is that the quantum Fourier transform ``extracts the period efficiently''. That is true, but it does not identify the resource: a classical machine also has a Fourier transform, and the fast Fourier transform is one of the best-understood algorithms in existence.

\subsection{Thesis}

Our thesis is that the Fourier mathematics is \emph{shared} and the entire difference lies in the input state. Both sides use the same primitive root $\zeta_n = e^{2\pi i/n}$ and the same character orthogonality on $\Zn$. The classical algorithm is restricted to sampling the \emph{value signal} $V(x) = a^x \bmod N$; the quantum circuit, after one coherent evaluation on a uniform superposition, holds the \emph{coherent comb}, the indicator of a residue class modulo $r$. We show that (i) the value signal is subject to two independent barriers, (ii) the comb has a perfect Dirac-comb spectrum from which the period is recoverable exactly, and (iii) the comb is uncertainty-extremal, so the advantage occupies the extreme point of a fundamental inequality.

\subsection{Honest scope}

We prove no superpolynomial lower bound on classical factoring; that problem is open. The barriers constrain a specific classical strategy --- Fourier sampling of the exponentiation signal. Barrier~1's sample bound is proved for linear measurement schemes; the nonlinear adaptive case is open (Section~\ref{sec:disc}). Barrier~2 is exact for order-$4$ instances and exhibited concretely; no asymptotic density claim is proved.

\section{The discrete Fourier transform used throughout}

Fix $n \ge 1$ and let $\zeta_n = \exp(2\pi i/n)$, a primitive $n$-th root of unity with $|\zeta_n^{\,j}| = 1$ for all $j$.

\begin{definition}[Transform and inverse]
For $v : \Zn \to \C$ set
\[
\hat v(k) = \sum_{x=0}^{n-1} v(x)\,\zeta_n^{\,xk},
\qquad
(\mathcal{F}^{-1}w)(x) = \frac1n \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} w(k)\,\zeta_n^{-xk}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Geometric character sum]\label{prop:geom}
If $\omega^n = 1$ and $\omega \neq 1$ then $\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\omega^{\,j}=0$; if $\omega = 1$ the sum is $n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$(\omega-1)\sum_{j<n}\omega^j = \omega^n - 1 = 0$ and $\omega - 1 \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inversion]\label{thm:inv}
$\mathcal{F}^{-1}\hat v = v$ for every $v$, provided $n$ is invertible in the coefficient field.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand and exchange sums; the inner sum $\sum_k \zeta_n^{(y-x)k}$ equals $n$ if $x=y$ and $0$ otherwise by Proposition~\ref{prop:geom}, since $\zeta_n^{\,y-x}$ is an $n$-th root of unity, equal to $1$ exactly when $x = y$ by primitivity.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:geom} is the \emph{single} engine used on both sides of the boundary below. Everything we prove about the ``quantum Fourier transform'' is a statement about this map.

\begin{definition}[Support]
For $v : \Zn \to \C$, $\supp v = \{x : v(x)\neq 0\}$.
\end{definition}

\section{The quantum side: the coherent comb}

\subsection{The state}

A Hadamard layer prepares $n^{-1/2}\sum_{x<n}|x\rangle|0\rangle$; modular exponentiation, applied once, yields $n^{-1/2}\sum_{x<n}|x\rangle|a^x \bmod N\rangle$; measuring (or tracing out) the second register collapses the first onto the set of $x$ producing the observed value, an arithmetic progression of step $r$.

\begin{definition}[Coherent comb]
Let $n = mr$ with $m,r \ge 1$ and $0 \le x_0 < r$. The \emph{comb state} is
\[
C_{m,r,x_0}(x) = \begin{cases} 1, & x \equiv x_0 \pmod r,\\ 0, & \text{otherwise},\end{cases}
\qquad x \in \{0,\dots,n-1\},
\]
equivalently the unnormalised superposition $\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}|x_0 + jr\rangle$, with transform
$\widehat C(k) = \sum_{j<m}\zeta_{mr}^{\,(x_0+jr)k}$. Note $|\supp C| = m$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Exact transform}

\begin{lemma}[Spacing collapse]\label{lem:spacing}
For $m,r\ge 1$, $\zeta_{mr}^{\,r} = \zeta_m$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\exp(2\pi i r/(mr)) = \exp(2\pi i/m)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Factorisation of the comb transform]\label{thm:combfact}
For $m,r\ge 1$ and any $x_0,k$,
\[
\widehat C(k) = \zeta_{mr}^{\,x_0k}\Big(\sum_{j<m}\zeta_m^{\,jk}\Big)
= \zeta_{mr}^{\,x_0k}\cdot
\begin{cases} m, & m \mid k,\\ 0, & m \nmid k.\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $(x_0+jr)k = x_0k + r(jk)$ and use Lemma~\ref{lem:spacing} to rewrite $\zeta_{mr}^{\,r(jk)} = (\zeta_m^{\,k})^{j}$. Factoring out the $j$-independent phase leaves $\sum_{j<m}(\zeta_m^{\,k})^{j}$. As $\zeta_m$ is primitive, $\zeta_m^{\,k}=1$ iff $m \mid k$; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:geom}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharp peak theorem]\label{cor:sharp}
$|\widehat C(k)| = m$ if $m \mid k$ and $|\widehat C(k)| = 0$ otherwise. The off-peak spectrum vanishes \emph{identically}, and $|\widehat C(k)| \le m$ always.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
$m$ is the maximum possible modulus of a sum of $m$ unit-modulus complex numbers, so the peaks saturate the triangle inequality: coherence is exactly the alignment of the $m$ phases. The peak height is independent of $x_0$, which contributes only a global phase --- this is why measuring the value register does no harm.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Peak count]\label{prop:peakcount}
$\#\{k < mr : m \mid k\} = r$; the peaks are $k = jm$, $0 \le j < r$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Equal harmonics]
$|\widehat C(j_1m)| = |\widehat C(j_2m)| = m$ for all $j_1,j_2$. No amount of peak \emph{ranking} singles out the fundamental, even in the perfectly coherent case.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Parseval bookkeeping]
$\sum_{k<n}|\widehat C(k)|^2 = r m^2 = nm$: the entire spectral energy sits on the $r$ peaks, each of weight $m^2$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{From a peak to the period}

\begin{theorem}[Period extraction]\label{thm:period}
Let $n = mr$ with $m,r\ge1$ and $\gcd(j,r)=1$. Then $\frac{jm}{mr} = \frac{j}{r}$ in lowest terms has denominator exactly $r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Cancel $m \neq 0$; since $\gcd(j,r)=1$ and $r>0$, the fraction $j/r$ is reduced.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Useful peaks]
The peaks $jm$ from which Theorem~\ref{thm:period} recovers $r$ are exactly those with $\gcd(j,r)=1$; there are $\varphi(r)$ of them, and at least one exists for every $r \ge 1$. In the exact-period model a single run therefore succeeds with probability $\varphi(r)/r = \Omega(1/\log\log r)$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Converse of the sharp-peak theorem]\label{thm:rigid}
Let $n = mr$ and $v : \Zn \to \C$ satisfy $\hat v(k)=0$ for every $k$ with $m \nmid k$. Then $v$ is $r$-periodic: $v(x+r)=v(x)$ whenever $x + r < n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By inversion $v(x) = \frac1n\sum_k \hat v(k)\zeta_n^{-xk}$, and only $k = jm$ contribute. For such $k$ the shift by $r$ multiplies the $k$-th term by $\zeta_n^{-rk} = \zeta_n^{-jn}=1$. Every surviving mode is $r$-periodic, hence so is $v$.
\end{proof}

Thus ``peak-supported spectrum'' and ``$r$-periodic'' are the same condition: the advantage cannot be recreated by a cleverer non-periodic state faking a sharp comb. Among $r$-periodic signals, the indicator comb has the smallest possible time support, $m$.

\section{Barrier 1: the classical sampling requirement}

\subsection{Resolution}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution bound]\label{thm:res}
If the linear measurement map $v \mapsto (\hat v(k_1),\dots,\hat v(k_K))$ is injective on the space of period-$r$ signals, then $K \ge r$. Equivalently, if $K < r$ there exist two \emph{distinct} period-$r$ signals with identical Fourier samples at those frequencies.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The space of period-$r$ signals is $\C^r$; the map is linear into $\C^K$. If $K<r$ the kernel is nontrivial, so some $u \neq 0$ has all samples zero and $v$, $v+u$ are indistinguishable.
\end{proof}

This is the discrete counterpart of ``frequency resolution $1/K$ must beat the spacing $1/r$''.

\subsection{Typical order is large}

\begin{theorem}[Small-order count]\label{thm:smallorder}
Let $G$ be a finite cyclic group and $B \ge 0$. Then
\[
\#\{g \in G : \ord(g) \le B\} \;\le\; B\cdot\#\{d : d \mid |G|,\ d \le B\} \;\le\; B^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every $g$ with $\ord(g)\le B$ lies in a root set $R_d = \{g : g^d = 1\}$ for $d = \ord(g)$, which divides $|G|$ and is $\le B$. In a cyclic group $|R_d| \le d \le B$. Summing over the index set gives the first bound; the second follows since the number of divisors of $|G|$ that are $\le B$ is at most $B$.
\end{proof}

The divisor-indexed form is a genuine refinement: for $|G| = p-1$ with $\tau$ divisors it yields a base of order $> |G|/\tau$, and $\tau = |G|^{o(1)}$ typically, hence $|G|^{1-o(1)}$ rather than $|G|^{1/2}$.

\begin{corollary}[Existence of a high-order base]
If $B\cdot\#\{d\mid|G| : d\le B\} < |G|$ --- in particular if $B^2 < |G|$ --- then some $g \in G$ has $\ord(g) > B$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Most bases have large order]
If $2B^2 < |G|$ then strictly more than half the elements of $G$ have order $> B$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Bases modulo a prime]\label{cor:prime}
For a prime $p \ge 3$, $(\Z/p\Z)^\times$ is cyclic of order $p-1$ and $\lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor^2 \le p-2 < p-1$. Hence some base $a$ has $\ord_p(a) > \lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor$; and whenever $2\lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor^2 < p-1$, a strict majority of bases do.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Barrier 1, assembled}

\begin{theorem}[Classical sampling barrier]
Let $p \ge 3$ be prime. There is a base $a$ modulo $p$ with $\ord_p(a) > \lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor$ such that every Fourier-sampling scheme determining the period signal uses more than $\lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor$ samples.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:prime} with Theorem~\ref{thm:res}: $K \ge r = \ord_p(a) > \lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Superpolynomiality]
With input size $x = \log N$, the requirement $\sqrt N$ is $x \mapsto e^{x/2}$, which exceeds every polynomial: $e^{x/2}/x^d \to \infty$ for each fixed $d$. The classical Fourier-sampling requirement is therefore not polynomially bounded in the bit-size.
\end{proposition}

\section{Barrier 2: spectral hiding in the value signal}

\begin{definition}[Value signal]
For $r = \ord_N(a)$, the \emph{value signal} is $V(x) = (a^x \bmod N) \in \C$, $x = 0,\dots,r-1$: what a classical algorithm actually observes.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The textbook instance}

For $N = 15$, $a = 7$ one has $7^2 \equiv 4$, $7^3 \equiv 13$, $7^4 \equiv 1 \pmod{15}$, so $r = 4$ and $v = (1,7,4,13)$. Since $\zeta_4 = i$:

\begin{theorem}[Exact bins]\label{thm:bins}
\[
\hat V(0) = 25,\quad \hat V(1) = -3-6i,\quad \hat V(2) = -15,\quad \hat V(3) = -3+6i,
\]
so $|\hat V(1)| = |\hat V(3)| = \sqrt{45}\approx 6.708$ and $|\hat V(2)| = 15$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation of $\sum_{x<4}v(x)\,i^{xk}$ using $i^2=-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The fundamental is dominated]
$|\hat V(1)| < |\hat V(2)|$, indeed $\sqrt{45} < \sqrt{225} = 15$; likewise for $|\hat V(3)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Peak picking returns a false period]
Among the non-DC bins the strict maximum is $k = 2$; reading the period off that peak gives $4/2 = 2$. But $7^2 = 49 \equiv 4 \not\equiv 1 \pmod{15}$ while $7^4 \equiv 1$. The largest-peak heuristic outputs a number that is \emph{not} the order, and the subsequent $\gcd$ step then produces nothing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The spectrum is spread]\label{thm:spread}
All four bins are nonzero; no frequency can be discarded a priori.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{A general criterion at order 4}

Let $r = 4$ and $v_i = a^i \bmod N$.

\begin{theorem}[Closed forms]
\[
\hat V(1) = (v_0-v_2) + (v_1-v_3)i,\qquad \hat V(2) = v_0-v_1+v_2-v_3 \in \mathbb{R},
\]
so $|\hat V(1)| = \sqrt{(v_0-v_2)^2+(v_1-v_3)^2}$ and $|\hat V(2)| = |v_0-v_1+v_2-v_3|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral-hiding criterion]\label{thm:criterion}
For any $N$ and any $a$ of order $4$ modulo $N$,
\[
|\hat V(1)| < |\hat V(2)| \iff (v_0-v_1+v_2-v_3)^2 > (v_0-v_2)^2+(v_1-v_3)^2,
\]
and the reverse strict inequality gives the reverse comparison. This is a decidable inequality between integers.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Verified instances]
The criterion holds --- the fundamental is dominated --- for $(N,a) = (15,7)$ with residues $1,7,4,13$ and $45 < 225$; $(15,13)$ with $1,13,4,7$ and $45<225$; $(20,13)$ with $1,13,9,17$ and $80<400$; $(39,31)$ with $1,31,25,34$ and $585<1521$. Each base genuinely has order $4$.
\end{corollary}

\paragraph{Computational evidence.} Exhaustive enumeration over all pairs $(N,a)$ with $N<500$, $\gcd(a,N)=1$ and $\ord_N(a)=4$ yields $1870$ instances, of which $684$ (approximately $36.6\%$) satisfy the hiding criterion. Spectral hiding is a bulk phenomenon in this slice, not an isolated pathology.

\paragraph{Interpretation.} The fundamental collapses when the residues pair up antipodally ($v_0 \approx v_2$, $v_1 \approx v_3$) while the alternating sum stays large --- exactly the behaviour of pseudorandom residues. Nothing in $a^x \bmod N$ makes the fundamental structurally favoured.

\subsection{What a genuinely single-peaked signal must be}

\begin{theorem}[Two-bin structure theorem]\label{thm:twobin}
Let $\omega$ be a primitive $n$-th root of unity and $v : \Zn \to \C$ have $\hat v(k) = 0$ for all $k \notin \{0,k_0\}$ with $k_0 \neq 0$. Then
\[
v(x) = \tfrac1n\big(\hat v(0) + \hat v(k_0)\,\omega^{-xk_0}\big),
\]
i.e.\ $v$ is a constant plus a single character --- a pure sinusoid.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fourier inversion, retaining the two surviving terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The value signal is not single-peaked]
For $N=15$, $a=7$ no frequency $k_0$ carries the whole non-DC spectrum, since by Theorem~\ref{thm:spread} all three non-DC bins are nonzero. By Theorem~\ref{thm:twobin} the value signal is not a constant plus one character: the period is genuinely distributed across the harmonics.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Coherence dichotomy]
The same transform, two inputs: the coherent comb has identically vanishing off-peak spectrum and attains the maximal modulus $m$ on its peaks, while the value signal for $N=15$, $a=7$ has no vanishing bin and its largest non-DC bin points at a false period. Sharpness is a property of the prepared state, not of the transform.
\end{corollary}

\section{Uncertainty-extremality of the comb}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete uncertainty principle, Donoho--Stark form]\label{thm:unc}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $v : \Zn \to \C$ be nonzero. With $A = \supp v$ and $B = \supp \hat v$,
\[
|A|\cdot|B| \;\ge\; n .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\|v\|_\infty = \max_x|v(x)|$, attained at $x_0$, and $\|\hat v\|_\infty = \max_k|\hat v(k)|$; $\|v\|_\infty > 0$ since $v \neq 0$.

\emph{Step 1.} For any $k$, since $|\zeta_n^{\,xk}| = 1$ and only $x \in A$ contribute,
$|\hat v(k)| \le \sum_{x\in A}|v(x)| \le |A|\,\|v\|_\infty$, hence $\|\hat v\|_\infty \le |A|\,\|v\|_\infty$.

\emph{Step 2.} By inversion, $v(x_0) = \frac1n\sum_k\hat v(k)\zeta_n^{-x_0k}$, and only $k \in B$ contribute, so
$\|v\|_\infty \le \frac1n|B|\,\|\hat v\|_\infty$.

\emph{Step 3.} Chaining, $\|v\|_\infty \le \frac1n|A||B|\,\|v\|_\infty$; multiply by $n>0$ and divide by $\|v\|_\infty>0$.
\end{proof}

The proof uses only unit modulus of the roots of unity, the triangle inequality, and inversion (Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}).

\begin{theorem}[The coherent comb is extremal]\label{thm:extremal}
Let $n = mr$ with $m,r\ge1$ and $0 \le x_0 < r$. Then
\[
|\supp C_{m,r,x_0}| = m,\qquad |\supp \widehat{C_{m,r,x_0}}| = r,\qquad m\cdot r = n,
\]
so equality holds in Theorem~\ref{thm:unc}: the comb saturates the uncertainty principle.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The time support is $\{x_0 + jr : j < m\}$, of size $m$ (using $x_0 < r$). The frequency support is the set of multiples of $m$ below $mr$, of size $r$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:sharp} and Proposition~\ref{prop:peakcount}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:unc} forbids simultaneous concentration in time and frequency, which is precisely the trade-off classical period finding fights: a short data record has small time support and therefore a spread spectrum --- the qualitative content of $K \ge r$. The quantum circuit does not fight the trade-off; it sits at its extreme point. With Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} the picture is complete: peak-supported spectra are exactly the $r$-periodic signals, and among these the indicator comb minimises time support and hence achieves extremality.
\end{remark}

\section{The boundary, in one statement}

\begin{theorem}[Quantum--classical boundary in period finding]
Fix a prime $p \ge 3$, a register size $n = mr$ with $m,r\ge1$, an offset $x_0 < r$, an index $j$ with $\gcd(j,r)=1$, and any $k$ with $m \nmid k$. Then simultaneously:

\emph{Classical side.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item there is a base $a$ modulo $p$ with $\ord_p(a) > \lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor$ for which every Fourier-sampling scheme determining the period signal uses more than $\lfloor\sqrt{p-2}\rfloor$ samples;
\item for $N=15$, $a=7$ the fundamental is strictly dominated, $|\hat V(1)| = \sqrt{45} < 15 = |\hat V(2)|$, so peak picking outputs a non-period.
\end{enumerate}

\emph{Quantum side.}
\begin{enumerate}\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item $\widehat C(k) = 0$ exactly (off-peak annihilation);
\item $|\widehat C(jm)| = m$ (maximal peak);
\item $|\supp C|\cdot|\supp\widehat C| = mr = n$ (uncertainty saturation);
\item $jm/n$ in lowest terms has denominator exactly $r$ (period recovered).
\end{enumerate}
Both sides use the same transform and the same character orthogonality. Only the input state differs.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Separation, sampling form]
Fix $r \ge 1$, $n = mr$ and $K < r$ frequencies. Then (i) there exist two distinct period-$r$ signals with identical Fourier samples at those frequencies, so the period is information-theoretically undetermined; while (ii) the single coherent comb has a peak of maximal amplitude $m$ from which the period $r$ is recovered exactly.
\end{theorem}

\section{Discussion and future directions}\label{sec:disc}

\subsection{What has been established}

The quantum advantage in period finding has been \emph{located}. It is not in the transform (shared), not in the post-processing (classical continued fractions), and not in a vague notion of parallelism. It is in the input state: one coherent evaluation of $a^x \bmod N$ on a uniform superposition yields a comb, and the comb is extremal for the discrete time--frequency uncertainty principle. The classical algorithm is confined to the value signal, which is expensive to sample to sufficient resolution and spectrally diffuse in a way that actively misleads peak picking.

\subsection{What has not}

No superpolynomial lower bound on classical factoring is claimed or implied; that remains open, and the number field sieve beats $\sqrt N$ comfortably by not being a period finder at all. Theorem~\ref{thm:res} concerns linear measurement schemes. Barrier~2 is exact for the order-$4$ family and documented by an exhaustive scan, not by an asymptotic theorem. The comb analysis assumes the exact-period regime $r \mid n$; with $r \nmid n$ the peaks broaden into Dirichlet kernels and the statements become approximate.

\subsection{Conjectures}

\begin{conjecture}[Uncertainty-extremality characterises the Shor input state]
Let $n = mr$ and $v \neq 0$ on $\Zn$ satisfy $|\supp v|\cdot|\supp\hat v| = n$. Then $\supp v$ is a coset of a subgroup of $\Zn$ and $|v|$ is constant on its support; that is, $v$ is a phase-modulated comb, so the Shor input state is, up to phases and translation, the \emph{only} extremal input.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that the two triangle inequalities in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:unc} are simultaneously tight exactly when all contributing phases align, forcing the support to be an arithmetic progression closed under translation --- a subgroup coset. The equality analysis is a finite-dimensional rigidity argument requiring no new analytic input; a counterexample would exhibit a non-comb state as sharp as the comb.

\begin{conjecture}[Peak-picking failure is generic]
For every $r \ge 4$ the density of pairs $(N,a)$ with $\ord_N(a) = r$ for which the fundamental bin is not the largest non-DC bin of the value signal is bounded away from $0$ as $N \to \infty$; in fact the fundamental's rank grows like $\Theta(r)$.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that the residues $a^x \bmod N$ behave like an equidistributed sequence with respect to the additive characters of $\Z/r\Z$, so the value spectrum is asymptotically flat and the fundamental has no structural advantage --- it is the comb, not the exponential, that produces sharpness. The order-$4$ slice is exactly solvable (Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}) with measured density $0.366$ for $N<500$; the general statement needs only Weyl-sum bounds for exponential sequences modulo $N$.

\begin{conjecture}[Nonlinear-estimator version of Barrier 1]
The bound $K \ge r$ survives dropping linearity: any (possibly adaptive, possibly nonlinear) classical estimator recovering the period of an arbitrary period-$r$ signal from $K$ evaluations of its Fourier transform at chosen frequencies must have $K \ge r$.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that the linear-algebra proof should be replaceable by an explicit two-point argument: exhibit, for each frequency set of size $<r$, a concrete pair of signals with distinct periods but identical samples --- which the kernel-dimension argument already provides non-constructively.

\begin{conjecture}[Divisor-refined order statistics]
Making $\tau(n) = n^{o(1)}$ explicit in Theorem~\ref{thm:smallorder} would upgrade Corollary~\ref{cor:prime} from ``order $> \sqrt p$'' to ``order $> p^{1-o(1)}$'', closing the gap to the heuristic $r = \Theta(N)$.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{A design lesson}

If the quantum advantage in period finding is precisely the ability to prepare an uncertainty-extremal state, then the natural question for any proposed quantum algorithm is: \emph{which extremal state does it prepare, and for which inequality?} Conversely, classical algorithms that simulate the preparation of a near-extremal state by structural insight rather than sampling should recover much of the advantage --- arguably what the number field sieve does in a different guise.

